          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-0965af8efc2630ce\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The embedded clause “je ne vous aimais plus” presents the cessation of the letter writer’s love as the content of the addressee’s settled belief. Its core force is representational and truth-conditional, even though embedded under “vous en étiez… persuadé.” It neither undertakes a relational commitment nor functions as an affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The passage distinguishes the addressee’s conviction from the writer’s calculated efforts to produce that conviction; this affects attribution and possible truth, but not the clause’s dominant truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"reported\",\"description\":\"Within a written letter, the writer reports that her addressee had become convinced that she no longer loved him; the highlighted clause expresses the reported belief-content rather than an independent direct declaration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The letter writer deliberately used withdrawal, strained reassurance, and dissimulation to make her lover perceive that her feelings had changed. The highlighted negative love proposition therefore occurs within an account of calculated emotional signaling and revenge. Whether her love had wholly ceased is less important to the classification than the fact that the clause represents that state as the content of his conviction.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“vous en étiez encore mieux persuadé que je ne vous aimais plus”\",\"supports\":\"The target is explicitly embedded as the propositional content of the addressee’s conviction, strongly supporting T and reported status.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je tâchais de vous rassurer; mais c'était d'une manière si forcée”\",\"supports\":\"Her forced reassurance unintentionally or strategically reinforced his belief; this is contextual manipulation rather than performative or expressive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She explains that she resolved to make him see that her feelings had changed while pretending to conceal that change.\",\"supports\":\"The wider strategy explains why the negative love-content is presented and perceived, without creating independent P or E force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as French cessative, negative, present/imperfect-form content with the temporal modifier “no longer.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the semantic core concerns cessation of a loving state rather than a positive commitment or exclamation.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the reported representation of a no-longer-existing loving state. The strategic, deceptive, and epistolary framing belongs to context and leaves no residual core force requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
